/*
    `::` yields true when the left operand is a collection all of whose elements are
    members of the right operand set.
    The left operand may be any collection, and in particular a multiset: duplicates are
    admitted and each contribution is tested on its own.
    That is exactly what separates `::` from `<=`. Both lift membership from a value to a
    collection of values, but `<=` relates two sets, which unify duplicate members, so it
    has no reading for a multiset on the left.
    `::` says nothing about order.
*/

{* 1, 2, 2, 3 *} :: int
